_qualified_callee composed _extract_callee_module -- the RAW path slot -- with
the callee name. An in-repo id carries a file path where an external one
carries a module, so

  python:src/app/views.py:10-20:handler:function  ->  src/app/views.py.handler

which no declared summary can equal. _module_from_symbol_path exists precisely
to normalise that (added by WI-damir for the same class of bug) and was sitting
sixty lines below in the same file. L53 applied to code rather than to a
measurement: when a production classification exists for the thing you are
computing, computing it yourself IS the bug. The function's own docstring
claimed it was "built from the two existing production extractors rather than a
fresh parse" -- true of the name half, false of the module half.

THE CONTROLS ARE THE LOAD-BEARING HALF of the new tests, because every key the
shipped catalogues declare is external: a normalisation that fixed in-repo ids
by breaking those would trade a dead capability for a live regression.
fmt.Printf, net/http.Get, go.uber.org/zap.String, os.path.join and logging.info
are all unchanged. The `external` placeholder now yields "" instead of the
fiction `external.print`, routing the callee to uncatalogued-therefore-unknown.

EFFECT ON THE SECTION 4 NUMBERS: DERIVABLY ZERO, and stated rather than
re-measured. The shipped catalogues declare only stdlib and third-party names,
so an in-repo key still matches nothing. Measured on a self-survey (141,494
edges, 72,822 call-like, 6,685 distinct callees): the key changes for 5,981
distinct callees / 64,177 call-edge instances, and catalogue hits are 0 before
and 0 after. Tracing whether a difference can reach the number, instead of
running a 16-minute cohort A/B to rediscover a null derivable from five lines,
is L59 applied in the direction it was learned.

KNOWN LIMITATION, INTRODUCED AND PINNED. The extension was an accidental
barrier: net/http.go.Get could not equal stdlib net/http.Get, and stripping it
removes that. 42 of 69 shipped qualified keys are shadowable in principle by a
repo-root file named after a stdlib module; zero collisions occur across the
72,822 measured call edges. A false "terminates" CLOSES a branch, so this
direction deletes findings. Latent while section 3a is confirm-only; the
provenance guard is filed as blocking WI-kabif rather than bolted on here, and
a test asserts current behaviour so a future guard fails loudly.

FIVE OF SIX SHIPPED PYTHON TERMINATING ENTRIES COULD NEVER FIRE.
python_stdlib.yaml keyed logging.Logger.{debug,info,warning,error,exception},
needing module slot `logging.Logger`. Across 64 logger-method call sites on the
self-survey the slots present are `warnings` (51), `external` (12) and one file
path -- not one carries logging.Logger, because `logger = getLogger(__name__)`
binds from a CALL expression and receiver resolution does not follow those.
Bare-module logging.* entries are added alongside; the Logger-qualified ones
are kept (correct, never consulted when unmatched, and they fire if receiver
resolution improves) with the split documented in the file instead of left as
silent dead data. Whether the bare form fires on the 9-repo cohort is
UNMEASURED -- it matches nothing on the self-corpus.

A DECAYING NUMBER IN A RATIONALE IS REPLACED BY AN EXECUTABLE TRIGGER. The
qualified-names-only safety argument rested on "11 of its 33 keys are such
aliases". That count moved 33 -> 108 -> 113 across two catalogue edits in two
days: L50's shape, a rationale going wrong with nobody deciding anything. The
count is gone from all three prose homes; the property it rested on -- that the
alias index holds bare colliding short names -- is now asserted by a test.

